        {
          "path": "_m/d/r/5/r/fraunces-tavern-wp/fraunces-tavern-museum.jpg",
          "filename": "Fraunces Tavern Museum.jpg",
          "caption": "\"Washington's Farewell\" — by sculptor Paul Manship (1885 – 1966); bronze relief plaque; no date.\nOn December 4, 1783, George Washington said goodbye to his officers in the Long Room at Fraunces Tavern, a meeting place  in New York City for the Sons of Liberty during the war.\nPaul Manship’s \"Washington's Farewell\" relief is one of several the sculptor created to commemorate episodes from the Revolutionary War (see also 1966.47.141, 1966.47.143, 1966.47.145 and 1966.47.136). Manship’s papers indicate that these reliefs were designed for a projected “Hall of History,” but research has not revealed where or when that project was to appear.\nIn the Smithsonian American Art Museum collection.",
          "alt": "\"Washington's Farewell\" — by sculptor Paul Manship (1885 – 1966); bronze relief plaque; no date.\nOn December 4, 1783, George Washington said goodbye to his officers in the Long Room at Fraunces Tavern, a meeting place  in New York City for the Sons of Liberty during the war.\nPaul Manship’s \"Washington's Farewell\" relief is one of several the sculptor created to commemorate episodes from the Revolutionary War (see also 1966.47.141, 1966.47.143, 1966.47.145 and 1966.47.136). Manship’s papers indicate that these reliefs were designed for a projected “Hall of History,” but research has not revealed where or when that project was to appear.\nIn the Smithsonian American Art Museum collection.",
          "width": 1600,
          "height": 1301,
          "mimeType": "image/jpeg",
          "license": "CC BY 2.0",
          "artist": "Cliff from Arlington, Virginia, USA",
          "sourceUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fraunces_Tavern_Museum.jpg",
          "sourceType": "commons-category",
          "sourceArticleId": "fraunces-tavern-wp",
          "sourceGeohash4": "dr5r",
          "smallPath": "_m/d/r/5/r/fraunces-tavern-wp/fraunces-tavern-museum-small.webp"
        },
